<script>(function(){var P=[["Akbar","/life/akbar","Life","1542 CE – 1605 CE","Akbar (1542–1605), born Jalal-ud-Din Muhammad Akbar, was the third Mughal emperor, who reigned from 1556 to 1605, enlarged the empire across much of the Indian subcontinent and governed it through a centralised administration and religious conciliation."],["Vedic civilisation","/culture/vedic-civilisation","Culture","c. 1500 BCE – c. 500 BCE","The Vedic civilisation of the northern Indian subcontinent, c. 1500–500 BCE, composed the Vedas, whose rituals, gods and social order became one of the main foundations of later Hindu traditions."],["Nzinga of Ndongo and Matamba","/life/nzinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba","Life","c. 1583 CE – 1663 CE","Nzinga Mbande (c. 1583 – 1663), called Ngola Njinga by her people, was queen of the Ambundu kingdoms of Ndongo and Matamba in present-day Angola, remembered for decades of war and diplomacy against Portuguese expansion."],["Guaraní","/culture/guarani","Culture","c. 1516 CE – present","The Guaraní are culturally related Indigenous peoples of South America, distinguished by the Guarani language, whose traditional range lies in Paraguay, Misiones, southern Brazil, Uruguay and Bolivia, and who number around 280,000 today."],["Elizabeth I","/life/elizabeth-i","Life","1533 CE – 1603 CE","Elizabeth I, the last monarch of the House of Tudor, was Queen of England and Ireland from 1558 to 1603; her reign saw an English Protestant church established, the Spanish Armada defeated and a flowering of English drama."],["Khoekhoe","/culture/khoekhoe","Culture","c. 400 BCE – present","The Khoekhoe are the Indigenous pastoralist peoples of southern Africa, herders of sheep and cattle since about the turn of the era, who lost their land to colonial conquest and live today as the Nama, Damara, Griqua and other nations."],["Babur","/life/babur","Life","1483 CE – 1530 CE","Babur (1483-1530), born Zahīr ud-Dīn Muhammad, was a Timurid prince of Fergana who lost his Central Asian lands, ruled Kabul and in 1526 founded the Mughal Empire in northern India; he is also remembered for his memoir, the Baburnama."],["Thracians","/culture/thracians","Culture","c. 1000 BCE – c. 600 CE","The Thracians were Indo-European-speaking peoples of south-eastern Europe, known from Homer to the 6th century CE for their horsemen and peltasts, gold and silver treasures and the Odrysian Kingdom."],["Augustus","/life/augustus","Life","63 BCE – 14 CE","Augustus, born Gaius Octavius and known as Octavian, was the founder of the Roman Empire and its first emperor from 27 BCE to 14 CE, whose principate opened the era of the Pax Romana."],["Indus Valley Civilisation","/culture/indus-valley-civilisation","Culture","c. 3300 BCE – c. 1300 BCE","The Bronze Age civilisation of present-day Pakistan and north-western India, whose planned cities, such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, flourished c. 2600–1900 BCE and whose script is still undeciphered."],["Yaa Asantewaa","/life/yaa-asantewaa","Life","c. 1840 CE – 1921 CE","Yaa Asantewaa, born around 1840 in Besease, was Queen Mother of Ejisu in the Ashanti Empire and led the Asante war against the British in 1900, the War of the Golden Stool; she died in exile in the Seychelles in 1921."],["Iberians","/culture/iberians","Culture","c. 600 BCE – c. 100 CE","The Iberians were an ancient people of the eastern and southern coasts of the Iberian Peninsula from the 6th century BCE, known for their oppida, the Lady of Elche, the falcata sword and their still unclassified language."],["Kublai Khan","/life/kublai-khan","Life","1215 CE – 1294 CE","Kublai Khan, a grandson of Genghis Khan, became Great Khan of the Mongols in 1260, founded the Yuan dynasty in 1271 and by 1279 completed the conquest of the Song, becoming the first non-Han emperor of all China."],["Lakota people","/culture/lakota-people","Culture","c. 1600 CE – present","The Lakota, or Teton Sioux, are a Native American people of the northern Plains who became horse-mounted bison hunters of the Black Hills, resisted the United States and live today on reservations in the Dakotas."],["Queen Victoria","/life/queen-victoria","Life","1819 CE – 1901 CE","Victoria (1819–1901) was Queen of the United Kingdom from 1837 and Empress of India from 1876, whose reign of more than 63 years gave its name to the Victorian era of industrial change and imperial expansion."],["Mississippian culture","/culture/mississippian-culture","Culture","c. 800 CE – c. 1600 CE","The maize-farming peoples of the Mississippi valley and the American South-East, c. 800–1600, whose chiefs ruled from towns of earthen platform mounds around plazas, Cahokia the greatest of them."],["Justinian I","/life/justinian-i","Life","c. 482 CE – 565 CE","Justinian I, called Justinian the Great, was Roman emperor at Constantinople from 527 to 565, remembered for reconquering North Africa and Italy, codifying Roman law in the Corpus Juris Civilis and rebuilding the Hagia Sophia."],["Tuareg","/culture/tuareg","Culture","c. 400 CE – present","The Tuareg are an Amazigh people of the Sahara and the Sahel, from Libya and Algeria to Niger, Mali and northern Nigeria, nomadic herders and caravan traders known for the men’s indigo veil and their resistance to French conquest."],["Martin Luther King Jr.","/life/martin-luther-king-jr","Life","1929 CE – 1968 CE","Martin Luther King Jr. (1929–1968) was an American Baptist minister and civil rights leader who led the Montgomery bus boycott, headed the Southern Christian Leadership Conference and used nonviolent protest against segregation until his assassination in Memphis."],["Yayoi culture","/culture/yayoi-culture","Culture","c. 300 BCE – c. 300 CE","The wet-rice farming culture that spread through the Japanese archipelago from northern Kyushu between about 300 BCE and 300 CE, bringing bronze and iron, permanent villages and the first polities named in Chinese records."],["Patrice Lumumba","/life/patrice-lumumba","Life","1925 CE – 1961 CE","Patrice Lumumba (1925–1961) was a Congolese politician and independence leader, the first prime minister of the First Congolese Republic in 1960, who was dismissed, arrested and executed by Katangan authorities within months of independence."],["Tocharians","/culture/tocharians","Culture","c. 200 BCE – c. 900 CE","The Tocharians, speakers of two Indo-European languages known from Buddhist manuscripts of the Tarim Basin, lived in oasis city-states such as Kucha and Agni on the Silk Road until their languages died out around the 9th century."],["Otto von Bismarck","/life/otto-von-bismarck","Life","1815 CE – 1898 CE","Otto von Bismarck (1815–1898), a Prussian Junker who became minister president of Prussia in 1862, unified Germany through three wars and governed the German Empire as its first chancellor from 1871 until Wilhelm II dismissed him in 1890."],["Oxus civilisation","/culture/oxus-civilisation","Culture","c. 2300 BCE – c. 1700 BCE","The Oxus civilisation, or Bactria–Margiana Archaeological Complex, was a Bronze Age urban culture of southern Central Asia (c. 2300–1700 BCE), known for walled towns such as Gonur Depe, fine metalwork and wide trade."],["The Buddha","/life/the-buddha","Life","6th or 5th century BCE","Siddhartha Gautama, called the Buddha, was a wandering religious teacher of the eastern Indo-Gangetic Plains in the 6th or 5th century BCE, remembered as the founder of Buddhism and its teacher of a Middle Way."],["Yangshao culture","/culture/yangshao-culture","Culture","c. 5000 BCE – c. 3000 BCE","A Neolithic farming culture of the middle Yellow River in China, c. 5000–3000 BCE, known for millet farming, ditch-enclosed villages such as Banpo and painted pottery with fish, face and geometric designs."],["Confucius","/life/confucius","Life","c. 551 BCE – c. 479 BCE","Confucius (c. 551 – c. 479 BCE), born Kong Qiu, was a Chinese philosopher and teacher of the Spring and Autumn period in the state of Lu, whose teachings on morality, family and government became Confucianism."],["Scythians","/culture/scythians","Culture","c. 800 BCE – c. 260 CE","Iranian-speaking horse nomads of the Eurasian steppe, famed as mounted archers, whose kings ruled north of the Black Sea from the 7th to the 3rd century BCE and were buried in great mounds rich in gold."],["Shaka","/life/shaka","Life","c. 1787 CE – 1828 CE","Shaka kaSenzangakhona (c. 1787 – 1828) was king of the Zulu Kingdom from 1816 to 1828, remembered for reorganising the Zulu army and for wars whose part in the Mfecane remains disputed."],["Haudenosaunee","/culture/haudenosaunee","Culture","c. 1450 CE – present","The Haudenosaunee, \"those who build the longhouse\", also called the Iroquois or Six Nations, are a confederacy of Iroquoian peoples of northeast North America, united under the Great Law of Peace and still governed by their Grand Council."],["Winston Churchill","/life/winston-churchill","Life","1874 CE – 1965 CE","Sir Winston Churchill was a British statesman, soldier and writer, Prime Minister of the United Kingdom from 1940 to 1945 and again from 1951 to 1955, remembered above all as Britain's leader in the Second World War."],["Nazca culture","/culture/nazca-culture","Culture","c. 100 BCE – c. 800 CE","The Nazca culture flourished on the desert coast of southern Peru from about 100 BCE to 800 CE, painting polychrome pottery, weaving fine textiles and making the Nazca Lines and the underground aqueducts called puquios."],["Nelson Mandela","/life/nelson-mandela","Life","1918 CE – 2013 CE","Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela (1918–2013) was a South African anti-apartheid revolutionary who spent 27 years in prison, negotiated the end of apartheid and served from 1994 to 1999 as the country's first black president."],["Bell Beaker culture","/culture/bell-beaker-culture","Culture","c. 2800 BCE – c. 1800 BCE","The Bell Beaker culture, c. 2800–1800 BCE, is an archaeologists’ name for a phenomenon of western and central Europe marked by bell-shaped beakers, copper daggers and archery gear, spread by trade and, in places, large migrations."],["Mansa Musa","/life/mansa-musa","Life","c. 1280 CE – c. 1337 CE","Mansa Musa (c. 1280 – c. 1337) was the ninth Mansa of the Mali Empire in West Africa, remembered for his wealth in gold, his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 and Timbuktu's growth as a centre of Islamic learning."],["Phoenicians","/culture/phoenicians","Culture","c. 1200 BCE – 332 BCE","The seafaring people of Tyre, Sidon, Byblos and their sister cities on the Lebanese coast, who founded Carthage and colonies across the Mediterranean and spread the alphabet from which Greek and Latin descend."],["Sundiata Keita","/life/sundiata-keita","Life","c. 1217 CE – c. 1255 CE","Sundiata Keita (c. 1217 – c. 1255) was a Mandinka prince who defeated Soumaoro Kanté of Sosso at Kirina and founded the Mali Empire, remembered above all through the Epic of Sundiata kept by the griots."],["Nok culture","/culture/nok-culture","Culture","c. 1500 BCE – c. 1 CE","A farming culture of central Nigeria, c. 1500 BCE to the turn of the Common Era, whose hollow terracotta figures are among the earliest sculpture known south of the Sahara and whose people smelted iron early."],["Moctezuma II","/life/moctezuma-ii","Life","c. 1466 CE – 1520 CE","Moctezuma Xocoyotzin (c. 1466–1520), known as Moctezuma II, was the ninth emperor of the Aztec Empire, which reached its greatest size under him, and was its ruler when Hernán Cortés and the Spaniards entered Tenochtitlan in 1519."],["Māori","/culture/maori","Culture","c. 1300 CE – present","The Māori are the Indigenous Polynesian people of New Zealand (Aotearoa), whose ancestors arrived by canoe around 1300 and built a culture of iwi and hapū, carving, pounamu and pā long before the Treaty of Waitangi in 1840."],["Rumi","/life/rumi","Life","1207 CE – 1273 CE","Jalāl al-Dīn Muḥammad Balkhī, known as Rumi, was a thirteenth-century Persian-language Sufi mystic and poet who lived in Konya in Anatolia; he is remembered for the Masnavi and as the figure from whom the Mevlevi Order descends."],["Vinča culture","/culture/vinca-culture","Culture","c. 5400 BCE – c. 4500 BCE","The Vinča culture was a Neolithic culture of the central Balkans, 5400–4500 BCE, known for some of the largest settlements of prehistoric Europe, dark-burnished pottery, figurines, the Vinča symbols and the earliest known copper smelting."],["Kamehameha I","/life/kamehameha-i","Life","c. 1736 CE – 1819 CE","Kamehameha I, also known as Kamehameha the Great, was a chief of Hawaiʻi Island who conquered the Hawaiian Islands, completed their unification in 1810 and ruled as the first king of the Kingdom of Hawaii until his death in 1819."],["Jurchens","/culture/jurchens","Culture","748 CE – 1635 CE","The Jurchens were Tungusic-speaking peoples of Manchuria, first recorded in 748, who founded the Jin dynasty (1115–1234) and, under Nurhaci, the Later Jin, before Hong Taiji renamed them Manchus in 1635."],["Ludwig van Beethoven","/life/ludwig-van-beethoven","Life","1770 CE – 1827 CE","Ludwig van Beethoven (1770–1827) was a German composer and pianist, born in Bonn and based in Vienna from 1792, whose symphonies, piano sonatas and string quartets carried music from the Classical style toward the Romantic, much of it written as his hearing failed."],["Sogdians","/culture/sogdians","Culture","c. 550 BCE – c. 1000 CE","An Iranian people of Central Asia, centred on Samarkand and Bukhara, whose merchants dominated the Silk Road trade with China from the 4th to the 8th century and whose alphabet lives on in the Mongolian script."],["Emperor Wu of Han","/life/emperor-wu-of-han","Life","156 BCE – 87 BCE","Liu Che, seventh emperor of the Han dynasty, ruled China from 141 to 87 BCE, a reign of 54 years in which the empire expanded to Central Asia, Korea and Vietnam and Confucianism became the state doctrine."],["Ancestral Puebloans","/culture/ancestral-puebloans","Culture","c. 1500 BCE – c. 1600 CE","The farming peoples of the Four Corners region of the American South-West, ancestors of today’s Pueblo peoples, who built the great houses of Chaco Canyon and the cliff dwellings of Mesa Verde."],["Socrates","/life/socrates","Life","c. 470 BCE – 399 BCE","Socrates (c. 470–399 BCE) was an Athenian philosopher who wrote nothing and is known through the accounts of Plato, Xenophon and others; he was tried for impiety and corrupting the youth and put to death by hemlock."],["Cimmerians","/culture/cimmerians","Culture","c. 1000 BCE – c. 630 BCE","The Cimmerians were an Eastern Iranic equestrian nomadic people of the Caspian and Ciscaucasian steppes who crossed the Caucasus in the 8th century BCE and shook Urartu, Phrygia, Lydia and the Neo-Assyrian Empire."],["Frederick Douglass","/life/frederick-douglass","Life","c. 1818 CE – 1895 CE","Frederick Douglass (c. 1818–1895) was an American abolitionist, orator, writer and statesman who escaped slavery in Maryland in 1838, wrote three autobiographies and became the leading figure of the African-American civil rights movement of the 19th century."],["Inuit","/culture/inuit","Culture","c. 1000 CE – present","The Inuit are Indigenous peoples of the Arctic from Alaska to Greenland, descendants of the Thule people who spread east from about 1000 CE, who survived colonisation and forced relocation and are today the majority in Nunavut and Greenland."],["Chandragupta Maurya","/life/chandragupta-maurya","Life","c. 320 BCE – c. 298 BCE","Chandragupta Maurya, who reigned from about 320 to about 298 BCE, founded the Maurya Empire in Magadha after defeating the Nanda dynasty, and took control of the eastern Indus Valley after Alexander the Great's campaign."],["Igbo-Ukwu culture","/culture/igbo-ukwu-culture","Culture","c. 850 CE – c. 1050 CE","The Igbo-Ukwu culture of south-eastern Nigeria, known from a shrine, a burial and a cache first dated to the 9th century CE, cast the region’s earliest known bronzes and traded for beads from beyond the Sahara."],["Joan of Arc","/life/joan-of-arc","Life","c. 1412 CE – 1431 CE","Joan of Arc (c. 1412–1431) was a French peasant's daughter who, claiming divine guidance, helped raise the siege of Orléans and secure the coronation of Charles VII during the Hundred Years' War, and was burned for heresy at Rouen."],["Tlingit","/culture/tlingit","Culture","c. 8000 BCE – present","The Tlingit, or Lingít, are an Indigenous people of the Pacific Northwest Coast, Alaska Natives and First Nations in Canada, known for their matrilineal clans, crest art and a hunting, fishing and gathering life built on rich fisheries."],["Isabella I of Castile","/life/isabella-i-of-castile","Life","1451 CE – 1504 CE","Isabella I, known as Isabella the Catholic (1451–1504), was Queen of Castile and León from 1474 who, with her husband Ferdinand II of Aragon, conquered Granada, expelled Spain's Jews and sponsored Columbus's voyage of 1492."],["Yamnaya culture","/culture/yamnaya-culture","Culture","c. 3300 BCE – c. 2600 BCE","The Yamnaya culture was a herding culture of the Pontic–Caspian steppe, c. 3300–2600 BCE, known for burial mounds and wagons and widely linked with the spread of Indo-European languages and ‘steppe ancestry’."],["Vladimir Lenin","/life/vladimir-lenin","Life","1870 CE – 1924 CE","Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov, known as Lenin, was a Russian Marxist revolutionary who led the Bolsheviks in the October Revolution of 1917 and headed the Soviet government until his death in 1924."],["Sanxingdui culture","/culture/sanxingdui-culture","Culture","c. 1700 BCE – c. 1000 BCE","A Bronze Age culture of Sichuan in south-west China, contemporary with the Shang dynasty, known from the sacrificial pits at Sanxingdui near Guanghan with their bronze masks, gold and jade in a style new to Chinese art."]],d=Math.floor(Date.now()/864e5),p=P[d%P.length],a=document.getElementById("hub-pick");if(a){a.href=p[1];var im=document.createElement("img");im.src="/logos/thumbs/"+encodeURIComponent(p[0]).replace(/'/g,"%27")+".png";im.alt="";im.width=64;im.height=64;a.querySelector(".hub-pick-img").appendChild(im);a.querySelector(".hub-pick-k").textContent="Today\u2019s pick \u00b7 "+p[2]+(p[3]?" \u00b7 "+p[3]:"");a.querySelector(".hub-pick-n").textContent=p[0];a.querySelector(".hub-pick-d").textContent=p[4];}var b=document.getElementById("hub-surprise"),P=["/life/","/culture/"];if(b)b.addEventListener("click",function(){function go(){if(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES==="undefined")return;var a=SEARCH_PROFILES.filter(function(r){return P.some(function(p){return r[1].indexOf(p)===0})});if(a.length)location.href=a[Math.floor(Math.random()*a.length)][1]}if(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES!=="undefined")return go();var sc=document.createElement("script");sc.src=(typeof SEARCH_PROFILES_SRC!=="undefined"?SEARCH_PROFILES_SRC:"/search-profiles.js");sc.onload=go;sc.onerror=go;document.head.appendChild(sc)});})();</script>
